  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nAs high pressure builds in across the Southwest, flow aloft \nremains mostly zonal, keeping conditions mostly dry and sunny. \nTemperatures will run 5-10 degrees above normal through the end \nof the work week. Deep daytime mixing will tap into stronger \nupper level winds, mixing down afternoon gusts of 20-30 mph. \nWith dry air in place, and daytime minimum humidities below 15%,\nthis will result in some localized critical fire weather \nconditions Thursday and Friday. \n\nModels continue to indicate a moisture push for the weekend, \nalthough discrepancies are cropping up in timing and intensity. \nA low is expected to swing into the Pacific Northwest Saturday, \ntracking across the Northern Rockies through the weekend. This \nwill help to shift flow back to southwesterly over eastern Utah \nand western Colorado. Ensemble guidance is indicating PWAT \nvalues of around 150% of normal over the weekend, although this \nis one discrepancy that is cropping up. The ECENS has a later \nand somewhat weaker moisture push compared to the GEFS. In \naddition, there is the question of forcing. All this moisture is\nno good if there's nothing to force showers and storms to form.\nAt this point, aside from favorable southwesterly orographics, \nthere is potentially a weak frontal boundary to work with. \nHowever, this boundary looks to weaken and become diffuse by \ntime it reaches eastern Utah and western Colorado, which will \nlimit its potential impact. If the front does hold together, we \ncould see shower and storm activity return. Either way, clouds \nand cooler temperatures are looking more and more likely for the\nweekend.",
